# THIS REPOSITORY GETS PUBLISHED.
#
# The presence of this file is the declaration. It is what switches on
# guard-publication-writes.sh and guard-publication-commits.sh, the two engine
# guards that refuse to let private material enter a tree that goes public.
# Delete it and this repository is unguarded — which is a visible, reviewable
# diff, and is meant to be.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS
# -------------
# On 2026-08-29 three measurement briefs and 137 asset files carrying full
# two-channel transcripts of the CEO's own webinar — a named third-party
# speaker, real business content, 28 verbatim quotes inside the brief prose —
# landed here across three consecutive lands. Each land was checked, by a human,
# against the rule "no media committed". Each check passed. The payload was
# TEXT.
#
#   THE PRIVACY QUESTION IS WHAT THE BYTES SAY, NOT WHAT FORMAT THEY ARE IN.
#
# The split used to be enforced by judgment at land time, on every commit,
# forever. This file is the point where it stops being judgment.
#
# RELATIONSHIP TO richos-hq/wiki/open-source-strategy.md
# -----------------------------------------------------
# That page is the STRATEGY: why the split exists, what the end state is, what
# the CEO decided. THIS FILE IS THE ENFORCEABLE RECORD — the only one any
# machinery reads. The two must not become two copies of one fact.
#
# The strategy page's directory table was measured against this tree before this
# file was written, and it does not survive as a predicate: it lists
# `docs/briefs/` under "stays private", while two files there are deliberately
# public today — README-transcription-work.md (added by the removal commit
# itself) and clark-brief-parakeet-viability-2026-08-29.md, a technology
# evaluation that quotes nothing. A guard derived from that table would block
# both, and a guard that blocks legitimate work gets switched off. So the
# boundary here is drawn on CONTENT, which is what the rule was always about,
# and on .gitignore, which already answers "will this be published?" per path.
#
# THE KEYS
# --------
# PRIVATE_RECORD    Named in every refusal, so the author is told where the
#                   content SHOULD go rather than merely that it may not go here.
#
# PRIVATE_SOURCES   Trees that hold private source material. The guard walks
#                   them and keeps the files that ARE private speech — the ones
#                   shaped like a recording, plus the ones that reproduce one of
#                   those in bulk, which is how whisper's plain .txt output
#                   (no timestamps, no speaker labels) gets in — then blocks any
#                   publication-bound write that reproduces >= MIN_QUOTE_WORDS
#                   of them verbatim. The operator declares WHERE private
#                   material lives; the machine works out WHICH files those are.
#                   No dated list of transcript paths to go stale.
#
#                   A file that merely QUOTES a recording does NOT join, and the
#                   thresholds that draw that line were measured rather than
#                   chosen: admitting mixed engineering documents puts their
#                   BOILERPLATE into the private corpus, and a corpus like that
#                   blocks LICENSE files. The numbers are in the engine's
#                   scripts/lib/publication-boundary.py.
#
#                   An entry INSIDE this repository must be gitignored — a
#                   "private" source that is itself published is not one, and
#                   the guard refuses rather than pretending otherwise. An entry
#                   that is simply not on this machine (a sibling private repo
#                   nobody cloned) is skipped, and every refusal names what it
#                   skipped so a block never overstates its own coverage.
#
# CORPUS_MAY_BE_EMPTY
#                   0 by default, and it should stay 0 here. When PRIVATE_SOURCES
#                   resolve to trees that exist but NOT ONE file in them
#                   qualifies as private speech, the corpus is empty — and the
#                   verbatim detector, the only one that catches speech quoted
#                   inside ordinary prose, then has nothing to compare against
#                   and would wave everything through while reporting clean.
#                   That is refused as BROKEN rather than passed as CLEAN. Set
#                   this to 1 only to state on the record that a repository
#                   genuinely has no private corpus yet; like ALLOWLIST it is a
#                   committed, diffable exemption and never an in-the-moment one.
#
# ALLOWLIST         Repository-relative path prefixes, space separated. The ONLY
#                   way through, and deliberately a committed one: the failure
#                   being fixed here was in-the-moment judgment, so there is no
#                   in-the-moment override token for these two guards.
#
# PRIVATE_FILES     Material that is private BY IDENTITY rather than by looking
#                   like a recording. Entries are `<64-hex-sha256>:<name>`, space
#                   separated, and THIS KEY MAY BE REPEATED — entries accumulate
#                   across lines, so declaring the next file is a one-line diff a
#                   reviewer can read at a glance. Every other key in this file
#                   refuses a second occurrence rather than letting the last line
#                   silently win.
#
#                   WHY IT EXISTS. The two detectors above are scoring functions,
#                   measured against transcripts and sharp for transcripts. A
#                   seven-line note about which typeface a wordmark was drawn in
#                   scores nothing at all, and on 2026-09-01 that file's exact
#                   bytes were written into this tree and staged and both guards
#                   returned 0. The instruction to keep it in the private record
#                   had been given twice by then, and twice it was honored by
#                   somebody remembering — which is the thing this file exists to
#                   stop depending on.
#
#                   MINT AN ENTRY, against the file where it actually lives:
#
#                     python3 <engine>/scripts/lib/publication-boundary.py \
#                         --digest <path-to-the-private-file>
#
#                   It prints the line to paste. The digest may be over the
#                   file's BYTES or over its normalized word sequence; the word
#                   form is what survives re-encoding, re-wrapping and a change of
#                   case, so it is the one the minter recommends for text.
#
#                   WHAT IS ENFORCED: the file does not enter this repository
#                   under another name (the digest travels with the content),
#                   reformatted or re-encoded (the word digest), into a gitignored
#                   path (an identity-declared file has ONE home and this is not
#                   it), or as a binary blob the text filters would otherwise
#                   drop. WHAT DEFEATS IT: a rewrite AND a rename together, or a
#                   partial excerpt under a new name. Either alone is caught.
#
#                   WHAT IT COSTS TO DECLARE ONE: the NAME becomes public, here,
#                   in a repository that gets published. The CONTENT does not — a
#                   sha256 is one-way. That trade is deliberate: a refusal that
#                   cannot say which file it means teaches nobody the rule, and a
#                   refusal nobody understands gets worked around. If a name is
#                   itself too much to disclose, the file does not belong in a
#                   declaration; keep it out of reach by other means.
#
#                   NOTHING HERE READS THE PRIVATE RECORD. The digest and the
#                   name are committed, so a fresh clone with no sibling checkout
#                   enforces exactly what this machine does — which is the
#                   machine that matters, because it is the one where nobody has
#                   heard the rule.

PRIVATE_RECORD="richos-hq (/Users/alex/ab/richos-hq) — the private HQ repository"

# docs/reference/local/ is the in-repo home for recordings and anything derived
# from them; it is gitignored, so nothing there is ever published.
# ../richos-hq is the private record itself: measured at 0.10s to walk in full,
# from which the shape filter keeps exactly the transcript files.
PRIVATE_SOURCES="docs/reference/local ../richos-hq"

# Both defaults are measured, not chosen — see scripts/lib/publication-boundary.sh
# in the engine for the numbers. Stated explicitly here so a reader of this file
# knows what is being enforced without going to look.
MIN_SPEECH_LINES=8
MIN_QUOTE_WORDS=10

ALLOWLIST=""

# 0 = an empty corpus is BROKEN. This repository has a private record, so an
# empty corpus here means the guard lost sight of it, not that there is nothing
# to see.
CORPUS_MAY_BE_EMPTY=0

